WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.950
A synthetic case: a user asks an exchange to send 500,000 USDT to an address.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:08.763
The address looks clean.

00:00:08.763 --> 00:00:09.800
But is the money?

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.619
A blacklist only knows addresses someone already reported.

00:00:14.619 --> 00:00:16.990
This one isn't listed, so it passes.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:20.538
ChainLens follows the money backwards.

00:00:20.538 --> 00:00:23.220
Two hops up: a scam's collection wallet,

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:28.070
fed by victims, split across mule wallets, and merged back in.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:33.280
On the demo site, both engines point the same way: hold.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:42.020
Two engines judge every request: a rulebook of known laundering patterns, and a model that reads the network's shape.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:47.110
A person makes the final call, with the evidence and a draft report in hand.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.530
The evidence: the path the money took.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:56.451
A newer trick: money that rests for minutes, then moves on.

00:00:56.451 --> 00:00:58.600
The rules alone would release it.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:04.475
The model spots the shape and asks for a closer look by a person: enhanced review.

00:01:04.475 --> 00:01:05.890
That's all it can do.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:09.730
It can't hold a withdrawal, or downgrade one, on its own.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:13.360
Released by the rules, flagged by the model.

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:17.322
And it mustn't punish the innocent.

00:01:17.322 --> 00:01:20.550
An exchange paying thirty users looks like laundering,

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.540
unless you know it's an exchange.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:25.702
Labelled: released.

00:01:25.702 --> 00:01:30.990
The system's own what-if says that without the label, all thirty would be held.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.466
Same withdrawal, two answers.

00:01:34.466 --> 00:01:40.910
ChainLens's screening model was trained and tested on synthetic scenarios, not real cases.
